  "worked_solution": "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+26x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{x}^{2}+13x+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{x}^{2}+13x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 4=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(3,4),\\,(6,2).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,12)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n1+12=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $x+12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+13x+4= \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x+12x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+x)+(12x+4) & = \\\\ x(3x+1)+4(3x+1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(3x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
